From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ysfaculty <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 22:33:23 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 09/11] hbbs: refuse a registration whose uuid or key is longer
 than any client's

Anyone can send RegisterPk over /ws/id, with no key and no ticket: in
enforce mode the answer is NOT_DEPLOYED, but its key is stored so that
hjv-api can bind a new PC. Upstream's message bound (64 KiB) and id bound
(253 bytes) came with the rebase; uuid and pk only had to be non-empty, so
each registration could still have hbbs keep up to 64 KiB of them in the
peer map, write them to db_v2.sqlite3 and log them whole.

What every HJViewer client from 1.9.0 on sends (hbb_common 229b904, the
pin of all of them): the uuid is get_uuid(), the machine id (on Windows
the MachineGuid, a 36-byte GUID) or the public key where there is none
and on Android and iOS; the key is the ed25519 public key of
Config::get_key_pair(), 32 bytes. handle_register_pk now refuses a uuid
over 128 bytes or a key over 64 with UUID_MISMATCH, as an id out of
bounds, before the IP blocker, the peer map, the database or the log hold
any of it, for TCP, WebSocket and UDP alike.

tests/untrusted_input.rs: registrations with a 254-byte id, a 129-byte
uuid or a 65-byte key (over WebSocket, and a uuid over UDP) are refused,
leave nothing in the files hbbs writes (database, journal, log) and no
key for hjv-pk, while registrations at the bounds and as clients send
them go through; an online query within upstream's bound is answered and
one past it ends the connection unanswered, on the WebSocket and the TCP
port; X-Real-IP in ::ffff: form counts against the IPv4 address and
reaches the peer as it, and without X-Real-Port the proxy's connection
port still tells clients behind one address apart.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
